    ("B59_9_1", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["Continuous Training"], "What is 'Catastrophic Forgetting' in Continuous/Online Learning?", "Catastrophic Forgetting occurs when a neural network is incrementally trained on a stream of new data (e.g., fine-tuning yesterday's model on today's logs). As the gradient descent optimizes the weights for the new data, it aggressively overwrites the weights that were critical for understanding the old data. The model learns the new patterns perfectly but completely forgets the historical patterns, causing accuracy to plummet on older, stable edge cases.", ["Occurs when a model is incrementally trained on a stream of new data", "Gradient descent overwrites weights critical for understanding historical data", "The model learns the new patterns but completely forgets the older, stable patterns"], ["It happens when the Data Scientist forgets to save the model"]),
    ("B59_9_2", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Continuous Training"], "Your Continuous Training pipeline fine-tunes your NLP model on daily user queries. On Friday, a massive spam bot attack floods the system. On Saturday, the model is completely corrupted and classifies everything as spam. How do you prevent a data anomaly from poisoning an online learning system?", "Online Learning systems are highly vulnerable to Data Poisoning. If bad data enters the stream, it is immediately baked into the model's weights. To fix this, you cannot train directly on raw streams. You must implement 1) Strict Anomaly Detection / Statistical filtering on the incoming data before it hits the training queue. 2) 'Replay Buffers': mixing the new daily data with a curated, pristine 'Golden Dataset' of historical data during the fine-tuning step to anchor the model and prevent catastrophic forgetting or poisoning.", ["Online learning is highly vulnerable to rapid Data Poisoning from spikes or anomalies", "Must implement strict upstream statistical filtering / anomaly detection on training data", "Must use a 'Replay Buffer' (mixing new data with a curated historical Golden Dataset) to anchor weights"], ["The model got scared of the bots"]),
    ("B59_9_3", "implement", "medium", "implement", ["Continuous Training"], "How do you implement 'Shadow State' monitoring to determine exactly when a batch model needs to be retrained?", "Instead of blindly retraining the model every week (which wastes compute and risks deploying a worse model), you implement a continuous monitoring trigger. You log the live production inputs and wait for the ground-truth labels to arrive. You continuously compute the production model's accuracy on the fly. Simultaneously, you calculate the Statistical Drift (PSI) of the incoming features. You configure a Webhook/Airflow trigger: ONLY when the rolling 7-day accuracy drops below an SLA (e.g., 85%), or the Data Drift PSI exceeds 0.2, the system automatically triggers a new training pipeline run.", ["Do not retrain on fixed schedules; it wastes compute and adds risk", "Continuously log live inputs, join with delayed ground-truth labels, and calculate real-time accuracy", "Trigger retraining ONLY when accuracy breaches an SLA or Data Drift exceeds a strict threshold"], ["You shadow the model by following it home"]),
    ("B59_9_4", "tradeoff", "hard", "tradeoff", ["Continuous Training"], "What is the tradeoff of using true 'Online Learning' (updating weights via Stochastic Gradient Descent on every single live user request) versus 'Micro-Batch Continuous Training' (retraining hourly)?", "True Online Learning adapts instantly to user behavior in milliseconds, providing the ultimate personalized experience (e.g., TikTok). The severe tradeoff is immense engineering complexity, extreme vulnerability to adversarial data poisoning (a user deliberately manipulating the model in real-time), and the inability to properly run evaluation metrics or rollbacks. Micro-Batching (hourly) provides a 1-hour delay in adaptation, but allows you to aggregate the data, run anomaly detection, run an automated validation test suite on the new weights, and safely promote the model using standard CI/CD.", ["True Online adapts instantly but is highly vulnerable to adversarial data poisoning and cannot be safely validated", "Micro-Batch (hourly) delays adaptation but allows for data sanitization, validation suites, and safe rollbacks", "Tradeoff: Instant personalization vs Safety, Security, and Validation"], ["Online learning requires the model to have a webcam"])
